  "summary": "Tempe councilmembers debated a first-reading ordinance that would make Tempe the first Arizona city to ban ALPR cameras, with sponsor Bobby Nichols pushing stricter limits over objections from two colleagues and Police Chief Ken McCoy. The city already canceled its Flock Safety contract last month and covered the cameras; a final vote is scheduled for Oct. 15.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The Tempe Police Department does not support that ban, and cannot support this ordinance as written. Since we decommissioned this technology, there have been crimes we have not been able to solve.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "The city canceled its contract with Flock Safety last month and covered the cameras citywide as it says the company has not responded to its removal request.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "Ultimately, it is an issue of the business model that these private companies use to make money off of our movements, and locations and data",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    }
  ],
